    <h1>Lists and Control Flow</h1>
    <p class="article-meta" data-pagefind-ignore>
      <span>2 min read</span>
    </p>
    <p>Lists hold ordered values of one element type. GCSL's loops are
expressions, so a loop can collect results into a list or run only for its
effects. This avoids a separate collection-building language.</p>
<h2 id="lists-and-ranges">Lists and ranges <a class="heading-anchor" href="#lists-and-ranges" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code> and <code>Int[]</code> name the same type. Indexes start at zero; a
negative or out-of-range index is a runtime error. A spread inserts
another list's elements. An empty list needs an expected element type:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let empty: List&lt;Int&gt; = []
let values = [...empty, 3, 8, 5]
values[1]
</code></pre></div>
<p><code>0..3</code> produces <code>[0, 1, 2]</code>; <code>0..=3</code> includes <code>3</code>. Bounds are integers and
ranges run upward. A range used directly as a loop's iterable is counted
without first allocating the list of all its integers.</p>
<h2 id="collecting-and-repeating">Collecting and repeating <a class="heading-anchor" href="#collecting-and-repeating" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A loop used as a value collects each iteration's result. A guard skips
iterations that don't match:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let doubled = for n in [1, 2, 3] if n &gt; 1 { n * 2 }
doubled
</code></pre></div>
<p>A loop used as a statement discards its results. Use <code>for condition</code> for
repetition while a condition holds, <code>for { ... }</code> without a condition,
or <code>do { ... } for condition</code> to test after the body. A header can contain
initialization and an update:</p>
<div class="code-block" data-language="gcsl"><button class="copy-code" type="button" data-copy-code><span class="copy-label">Copy</span></button><pre><code>let values = for let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i := i + 1 { i * 2 }
values
</code></pre></div>
<p>A plain header variable can be rebound by the header. Write <code>let mut</code> if
the body must also update it.</p>
<h2 id="leaving-a-computation">Leaving a computation <a class="heading-anchor" href="#leaving-a-computation" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p><code>break</code> ends the enclosing loop and <code>continue</code> skips to its next
iteration. Either can supply a value to the collecting loop before
leaving that iteration. An unlabeled <code>return</code> returns from the enclosing
function or <code>lazy</code> body.</p>
<p>Labels identify an outer loop or a block. A labeled <code>return</code> leaves that
target with one value; it does not append to a loop's accumulated list.
Control flow cannot jump out through a function boundary. See <code>gcsl(5)</code>
for label syntax and the distinction between collected and returned
values.</p>

